    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `LinuxDriver.start_application_mode`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `start_application_mode` function in the `LinuxDriver` class is responsible for preparing the terminal for application mode, which involves setting up signal handlers, configuring terminal attributes, and enabling various terminal features like mouse support and bracketed paste. It first checks if the file descriptor is associated with a terminal. If so, it sets up signal handlers for `SIGTTOU` and `SIGTTIN` to pause the application when the terminal is backgrounded. It then performs a no-op terminal attribute change to detect if the terminal is still writable after a background operation. The function initializes an asynchronous event loop and sets up a thread to handle terminal resizing events. It switches to the alternate screen buffer, hides the cursor, and enables focus tracking and keyboard protocol enhancements. It also configures terminal input settings to ensure single-character input and starts a thread for handling keyboard input. Finally, it signals the application that it has resumed if necessary. The function relies on helper methods like `_patch_lflag`, `_patch_iflag`, `_run_input_thread`, and `_request_terminal_sync_mode_support`. It handles exceptions by ignoring terminal attribute errors and returning early if a critical operation fails. Side effects include modifying terminal settings, starting threads, and sending asynchronous messages to the application.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
